PARS = [
("Generating alternatives is the weak point", """Decision analysis takes the set of alternatives as given and concentrates on evaluating it {35;36}, and this is where decision support under time pressure is weak. Option generation is a neglected phase that precedes choice {18}, it can be formalized as a recursion of decision problems {19;37}, and systems that hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not adopted {20}. Cognitive evidence agrees. Generating options in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval and raises the quality of the best option {38}, and operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation and filter them by a strict, external evaluation."""),
("Three kinds of mechanism exist", """The mechanisms that generate alternatives share one pattern. Some search a fixed model for solutions that differ in structure {21;22}, some combine modules under compatibility constraints {17;39}, and some add new actions to the action model at run time {40;41}. In every case the new alternatives are built from parts the system already understands, so an open decision space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis. The mechanisms that change a rule base at run time follow the same pattern. Evolving TS models tune and add rules within the vocabulary {33;34}, value-based selectors learn when a structural change is needed {42}, and generative models draft rules outside the current structure {43}. VODA uses all three, tuning and selection in the closed layer and generation in the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {44}, since generation must be cheap to reject and the evaluator must be independent of the generator."""),
("Recent wildfire and disaster systems", """Recent wildfire and disaster systems add learned and generative components but keep the intervention space closed or unchecked. Dubey and Dubey {45} map a Transformer forecast to four fixed response levels through expert rules. Chandra et al. {46} draw actions from a fixed ontology and check only their consistency. Cheng et al. {47} let LLMs weight a fixed set of indicators. Gerolimos et al. {48} call for open-world learning, but only for recognizing unseen events, not for responding to them. Multi-agent LLMs write free-text plans whose hallucinations are contained by retrieval, schemas, or post-hoc grading {49;50}. Cui et al. {51} argue for verification by physics-based simulation, yet confine their agent to a fixed tool library. WildfireKR {52} automates calibrated spread simulation but leaves intervention design to the analyst. None builds new interventions from effects a physical model can execute and lets that model decide admission. VODA fills this gap."""),
]
